# Musiclyse 0.6-dev3 — simultaneous fallback drums, fixed-boundary role labels, instrument evidence fixes.
# Legacy fallback structure/tempo evidence retained by default; recurrence label repairs opt-in.
"""
Musiclyse — chat about (and compare) multiple songs,
using a local LLM (via Ollama) to write the final response while a multi step pipeline performs grounded audio analysis on demand.

Routing:
    "/listen <question>" — analyzes the CURRENT track.
    "/listen <path or URL> <question>" — SWITCHES the current track to the
        given file/URL, then analyzes it. Each track keeps its own MOSS-Music
        conversation history, so switching back to an earlier
        track later still has that context.
    Anything else — goes straight to the LLM alone (general questions,
        comparisons between tracks already discussed, etc.), with the full
        conversation history — including every track's analysis so far.

Examples:
    /listen /Users/me/Music/song_a.mp3 what key and tempo is this in?
    /listen what instruments do you hear?              (still song_a)
    /listen /Users/me/Music/song_b.wav describe this one
    how does song_b's tempo compare to song_a?          (straight to the LLM)

New commands:
    /save=filename.json   Save the technical details for the most recently scanned track.
    /load filename.json [question]   Load a saved track; optional question after the name.
    /batch /path/to/folder   Overnight-scan every audio file in a folder into saved-songs/
                             as "Artist Name - Song Name.json" (from tags; Unknown +
                             original filename if tags missing). Same analysis as
                             /listen; does not import into chat.
    /clear                   Wipe chat context and reset session token counters.
    /clearall                Wipe chat context AND all in-memory track analyses
                             (saved-songs/ .json files on disk are kept, so /load
                             still works afterwards).
    /persona <description>   Switch chat voice/taste (music evidence rules stay).
    /persona reset           Restore the default music-obsessed friend persona.

Prerequisites:
    brew install ollama ffmpeg
    # Optional but recommended for Essentia on macOS:
    #   conda create -n musicalyse python=3.10 && conda activate musicalyse && conda install -c conda-forge essentia
    ollama serve                       # or run the Ollama app
    ollama pull muse-glimmer:30b-mlx   # the exact model named in OLLAMA_MODEL; if you
                                       # change OLLAMA_MODEL, pull that name instead
    pip install requests librosa mutagen
    pip install torch transformers     # MOSS-Music-8B-Instruct (listening model) and Whisper
    # MOSS-Music-8B-Instruct (~18 GB) downloads from Hugging Face on first use.
    # Its model code comes from OpenMOSS's GitHub repo (the Hub copy of the code
    # does not load with transformers) -- clone it once, at the tested commit:
    git clone https://github.com/OpenMOSS/MOSS-Music.git ~/MOSS-Music
    git -C ~/MOSS-Music checkout ad107c7ddaa06de168a0dfbc18d3e1e6a40c0e5e
    # (or clone it elsewhere and set MOSS_MUSIC_CODE_DIR). Nothing in it needs
    # installing: the script imports its src/ folder directly.
    # Its code targets transformers 4.57; the script adapts it to transformers
    # 5.x when it loads (see _moss_transformers_compat), so the transformers 5.x
    # you already have is fine. torchaudio and accelerate must be installed
    # (MOSS-Music's processor imports torchaudio; Demucs already needs it).

Optional stem/MIDI stack:
    pip install demucs omnizart tensorflow pretty_midi

Optional chord progressions (dev45, ENABLE_CHORD_PROGRESSION): one package,
which brings its own model (MIT licence):
    pip install lv-chordia

Optional finer genre styles (dev42, ENABLE_DISCOGS_STYLES): uses the torch and
transformers above; download its model (~350 MB) once with
    python musiclyse_style_setup.py

Optional per-stem/whole-mix instrument tagging AND independent genre/mood
cross-check (same pretrained tagger powers both; silently skipped if not
installed):
    pip install panns-inference
    # First use downloads a pretrained AudioSet checkpoint (~300MB) to
    # ~/panns_data/ automatically.

Usage:
    python musiclyse.py                     # no starting track — pick one with /listen
    python musiclyse.py /path/to/song.m4a   # optional starting track
"""

# ==============================================================================
# CHANGES IN 0.5-moss-dev46 (chat guidance fixes; nothing measured changes)
# ==============================================================================
# * BEAT_DRAWING_NOTE (on): "draw the drum beats" with no part named gets one
#   grid per part whose groove differs (up to BEAT_DRAWING_MAX = 4), each
#   labelled, instead of only the first grid in the notes.
# * FOLLOWUP_QUESTION_NOTES (on): follow-up questions about the current track
#   get the same question-specific notes as the first question (era, chords,
#   melody, lyrics, instrument dynamics, beat drawing, wording check). Before,
#   only the genre and structure notes reached them.
# * SOUND_SOURCE_NOTE (on): the sound is described from the measurements only;
#   knowledge of the song, album or artist stays separate casual context. The
#   era note says plainly when the measured era differs from the release date;
#   style percentages and words like "style readings" are kept out of answers.
# * IDENTITY_FLIP_NOTE (on): when the first vocal pass and the voice
#   descriptions heard a female voice and only the confirmation pass heard an
#   adult man, the singer-identity step decides from tags, cover art and
#   knowledge instead of deferring to that male reading (Dancing Queen was
#   saved as a male lead). Other tracks' identity prompts are unchanged.
#   Needs a rescan of the affected tracks.
# * TEMPO_COPY_CHECK (on): a listening-model TEMPO_BPM that is a copy, to the
#   decimal, of a detector reading it was shown no longer counts as a second
#   opinion in a half/double-time choice (Dancing Queen came out at 200 BPM).
#   Saved scans are corrected when loaded.
# * The wording check also keeps "the file is tagged", "the file you've got",
#   "the cover you've got", "the clip", stems and measurements out of answers.
# * The system prompt, the Wikipedia lookup and every other measurement are as in dev45.
#
# ==============================================================================
# CHANGES IN 0.5-moss-dev45 (chord progressions, melody in the key, measured melodicism)
# ==============================================================================
# * ENABLE_CHORD_PROGRESSION (on; needs "pip install lv-chordia" once -- MIT
#   licence, PyTorch, model included): a chord-recognition model (Jiang et al.,
#   ISMIR 2019) reads the full mix's chords in time order in the background
#   signal processing (CPU). New CHORD PROGRESSION BY SECTION block: each
#   section's progression with its repeating cycle folded ("A - D/F# - E - F#m,
#   four times"), Roman numerals against the recommended key (I - IV - V - vi),
#   how often the chords change, chords outside the key and the song's most
#   common cycle. Chord segments are saved in the .json. Needs a rescan; scans
#   without it are unchanged. Not installed: skipped with a one-line note.
# * MELODY_IN_KEY_BY_SECTION (on): per section, the notes the lead line's held
#   notes centre on, where its phrases come to rest and its peak, as degrees of
#   the key. Built from the transcription already in every scan (saved scans get
#   it on /load). Only held notes count and a part whose notes do not fit the
#   key is not described in key terms, because short notes are often
#   transcription slips.
# * MELODICISM_MEASURED (on): a grounded reading of how melodic the voice is,
#   overall and part by part, from its range, its share of long held notes and
#   the word rate of the timed lyrics (9 of 11 rap tracks rhythm-led, none of 9
#   ballads). Saved scans get it on /load.
# * WRITER_RULES_DEV45 (on): the chat model's rules now cover every block added
#   since dev42 (style signal, regional labels, ERA CHECK, chord progressions,
#   melody in the key, melodicism), each applying only when its block is
#   present; chord and melody questions get a matching note. Off: dev44's
#   system prompt byte for byte.
# * Nothing else changes: section maps, key, tempo, genre, vocals and every
#   existing block are as in dev44 (checked on all 230 saved scans).
# ==============================================================================
# CHANGES IN 0.5-moss-dev44 (style-led genre, era check, regional labels)
# ==============================================================================
# Tuned and checked on the MAEST probe of 120 of your songs against reference
# genres and original release years (five rotating held-out groups).
# * GENRE_STYLE_LED (on): the recommended genre follows the clearest measured
#   style where better supported -- "rock" -> "hard rock", "indie pop" ->
#   "disco" (Dancing Queen, Rivers of Babylon), "synth-pop" -> "gothic rock"
#   (a gothic-rock test track), "pop-punk" -> "nu metal" (In the End). Exact genre
#   60% -> 73%, right family 97% -> 99%; one song moves to a label the reference
#   lacks (Born in the U.S.A.: rock -> hard rock).
# * ERA_STYLE_CHECK (on): an era estimate of "2010s-2020s" that a confident,
#   period-bound style contradicts is replaced by that style's decades (Fire and
#   Rain 2010s-2020s -> 1960s-1970s). 43% -> 53% of era ranges right, none
#   made wrong.
# * REGIONAL_GENRE_GATE (on): K-pop, J-pop, Europop, Britpop, Krautrock, Latin,
#   Bollywood and other place-named labels only for lyrics in that language or
#   an artist the local Wikipedia copy calls a native ("is a South Korean ...");
#   World / International / "Asian" / "African" / "Middle Eastern" never; a song
#   not sung in English is never called world music for that reason. The style
#   block's "Folk, World, & Country" family is shown as "Folk / Country".
# * All three change only the chat model's view of the notes (and the GENRE
#   CONSISTENCY note): the analysis, the saved .json, the section map, key,
#   tempo and everything else are exactly dev43's. Each False restores dev43.
# ==============================================================================
# CHANGES IN 0.5-moss-dev43 (chat: the genre rules know about the style tags)
# ==============================================================================
# dev42's DISCOGS STYLE SIGNAL was not covered by the chat model's genre rules,
# and the GENRE CONSISTENCY note sent with genre, style, era and instrument
# questions ("every genre word must agree with the recommended genre") would
# have kept it from using a style the block names, and "is it X?" checked only
# the listening model's own lists.
# * GENRE_STYLE_NOTE (on): when the track's notes have a style block with at
#   least one clear style, that note also says the styles are measured from the
#   recording, a closely related one may be named alongside the recommended
#   genre (and counts as agreeing with it), "is it X?" with X among the styles
#   is at least "partly, yes", a style from another family is only an
#   alternative reading, the recommended genre stays the main label, and the
#   classifier and Discogs are never named. New scans' style block also says
#   not to name them.
# * Without a style block (every scan made before dev42, or with the model not
#   installed) the notes, the system prompt and everything else are exactly
#   dev42's. Checked on all 226 saved scans with 8 kinds of question. False
#   restores dev42.
# ==============================================================================
# CHANGES IN 0.5-moss-dev42 (Discogs style tags: a third, finer genre signal)
# ==============================================================================
# * ENABLE_DISCOGS_STYLES (on): MAEST, a music classifier trained on Discogs
#   (Music Technology Group, UPF; the same group as Essentia's Discogs-400),
#   names the record-shop styles a recording sounds like, from 400: Disco,
#   Europop, Beat, Psychedelic Rock, Glam, New Wave, Britpop, ... The listening
#   model's genre answers mostly come from a few stock lists, and the PANNs tags
#   have about 40 broad labels; this adds the finer styles.
# * It runs on the PyTorch/transformers already installed (no TensorFlow, no
#   change to Essentia), on the CPU, in the background signal-processing step:
#   a few seconds per song, about 0.5 GB while it runs, freed straight after.
#   Its input spectrogram is computed here exactly as its published feature
#   extractor does, so no downloaded code runs.
# * Shown as a DISCOGS STYLE SIGNAL block after RECOMMENDED GENRE FOR
#   DISCUSSION, and saved in the song's .json ("discogs_styles": the 40
#   strongest). Additive only: the recommended genre, era estimate, tempo, key
#   and every other result are computed exactly as in dev41; the block is
#   placed after the genre decision and none of the checks that read the
#   analysis text react to it.
# * One-time download (~350 MB): python musiclyse_style_setup.py. Until then,
#   and if the model cannot be loaded, it is skipped with a one-line note and
#   the scan is exactly dev41's. False restores dev41.
# ==============================================================================
# CHANGES IN 0.5-moss-dev41 (memory: free the listening model before Whisper)
# ==============================================================================
# dev40's readouts showed 18-27 GB of the listening model's memory still held
# by the Apple GPU cache when Whisper loaded, and releasing the cache alone
# (dev40) did not free it until Whisper was unloaded. Two causes, two fixes:
# * MOSS_RELEASE_REFRESH_LOCALS (on): on Python 3.11/3.12 the analysis
#   function's earlier locals() calls keep a snapshot of its variables, so the
#   listening model was still referenced when its unload clean-up ran. The
#   snapshot is now refreshed right after the model is dropped (batch scans and
#   /listen). Python 3.13 was not affected.
# * WHISPER_RELEASE_BEFORE_LOAD (on): just before Whisper loads and after each
#   Whisper pass, the full clean-up the unload routines use (garbage collection,
#   wait for the GPU, release its cache), and a wait for the GPU before each
#   in-pass cache release. Main thread only.
# * With WHISPER_PROGRESS on, "MOSS-Music unloaded" now shows the memory readout
#   too, so the effect can be seen. Display only.
# Memory only: transcriptions, analysis results and section maps are unchanged.
# The listening model's own settings (PYTORCH_MPS_* watermarks,
# MOSS_MPS_CACHE_TRIM_EVERY, RELEASE_MOSS_LOCALS_BEFORE_UNLOAD and the unload
# routines) are unchanged. Both new settings False restores dev40.
# ==============================================================================
# CHANGES IN 0.5-moss-dev40 (Whisper progress, memory readout, GPU cache release)
# ==============================================================================
# The Whisper stage looked hung after "Whisper loaded on device": it was
# decoding the whole song two or more times with nothing on screen.
# * WHISPER_PROGRESS (on; False restores dev39's messages): each Whisper pass
#   is shown as it runs ("Whisper pass 2: cross-check (sampled)...") with its
#   time, and a memory readout follows loading, each pass and unloading: this
#   process, Apple GPU memory in use and cached, the Omnizart helper process,
#   and the Mac's free memory. Display only.
# * WHISPER_MPS_CACHE_TRIM_EVERY (256; 0 restores dev39): on Apple GPUs the
#   cache of freed GPU memory is released every 256 tokens Whisper generates
#   and after each pass, as MOSS_MPS_CACHE_TRIM_EVERY does for the listening
#   model. Main thread only; only freed memory is released. Should a
#   transformers version ever reject the release step, that pass runs again
#   without it and the step stays off for the session.
# Checked with the real transformers pipeline (4.46, 4.57, 5.20) on a small
# Whisper model: transcriptions identical to dev39 in every pass type; with
# both settings off, messages and behaviour exactly as dev39. The listening
# model's memory settings (PYTORCH_MPS_* watermarks, MOSS_MPS_CACHE_TRIM_EVERY,
# the unload routines) are unchanged. Section maps are unchanged on all 218
# saved scans.
# ==============================================================================
# CHANGES IN 0.5-moss-dev39 (tempo: two measurements an octave apart decide)
# ==============================================================================
# Only the tempo decision changed (reconcile_bpm; see TEMPO_MEASURED_OCTAVE_PAIR).
# When Essentia and the beat tracker are an exact octave apart and the
# listening model's own figure matches neither, the two measurements decide
# between themselves; before, the model's figure could be paired with one of
# them as "~2x" (any ratio 1.8-2.2) and kept. Checked on 106 songs with a
# published or Harmonix tempo: exact 83% -> 86%, wrong 8% -> 5%, no song worse.
# 9 of 189 saved songs would get a new tempo on a new scan; saved files keep
# theirs when loaded (the load-time tempo view is unchanged on all 189).
# Section maps: unchanged for 8 of the 9; Times Like These 75.7% -> 76.0%.
# ==============================================================================
# CHANGES IN 0.5-moss-dev38 (audit of the section rules added since dev25)
# ==============================================================================
# Every section rule added since dev25 was checked on its own against all the
# reference songs (see "dev38 settings"). Three that never helped are off:
# STRUCTURE_ONEOFF_VERSE_TYPICAL, STRUCTURE_LEARNED_NAMER (never ran: no table
# file) and STRUCTURE_KNOWN_PART_WORDS_VETO (no effect on any saved scan).
# 4 of 216 saved scans change: Rock That Body 26% -> 42%; Beautiful Life same
# labels, section lines 0.60 -> 0.57; Head Like a Hole has no reference.
# Tried and not kept (no gain on the reference songs): moving section lines to
# where the singing starts, to vocal phrase starts or to whole 4-bar phrases,
# and splitting sections of 40 s or more.
# ==============================================================================
# CHANGES IN 0.5-moss-dev37 (section lines by repetition; pre-chorus; last chorus)
# ==============================================================================
# Tested on 216 saved scans against references timed by ear: your timings
# (22 songs) and Harmonix annotations lined up with each scan (69 songs), in
# five rotating folds. Every switchable section rule was also switched in turn;
# only these three changes improved the songs without dropping any song timed
# by ear. Label match: the 29 new Harmonix songs 67.9% -> 69.9%, your songs
# 81.7% -> 82.4%, all Harmonix songs 67.6% -> 68.5%; 10 songs better, 1 worse
# (U Can't Touch This, 59% -> 57%). 200 of the 216 scans keep dev36's map.
# * STRUCTURE_KNOWN_PART_NOT_HALVES (on): no section line by repetition in the
#   exact middle of a segment (a part singing its phrase twice).
# * STRUCTURE_PRECHORUS_PROPAGATE (on): a pre-chorus found by its words in one
#   verse is looked for, by those words, in the other verses.
# * STRUCTURE_FINAL_CODA switched off: it cut the last chorus short.
# ==============================================================================
# CHANGES IN 0.5-moss-dev36 (beat grid at half or quarter tempo; Whisper timing check)
# ==============================================================================
# Tested on 179 saved scans, against references timed by ear: your timings
# (20 songs) and Harmonix annotations lined up with each scan by their beats
# (38 songs; 5 more left out because their beats did not line up), split into
# a half used while working and a half held out.
# * STRUCTURE_REGRID_OCTAVE_SLOW (on): when the beat grid runs at half or a
#   quarter of the decided tempo, each beat is split before the section map
#   is measured. 13 of 30 new scans had such a grid; their bars were two or
#   four bars long, so sections came out double length. Label match: working
#   half 68.7% -> 71.6%, held-out half 68.9% -> 69.8%; section lines 0.728 ->
#   0.764 and 0.694 -> 0.700; no song timed by ear got worse.
# * WHISPER_WORD_TIMING_DENSE_WPS (on, new scans only): lyric timing also
#   counts as collapsed -- and the words are re-timed -- when Whisper crams
#   40 real words or more into a line lasting next to no time (one scan: 76%
#   of a song's words in a line 0 s long). Changes the check on exactly the
#   two saved scans that have this fault and no other.
# * STRUCTURE_TITLE_SEGMENTS_NEEDS_NEW_WORDS (on): the dev32 title rule only
#   for songs whose parts do not repeat their words, as it was meant. It had
#   renamed a post-grunge song's chorus Verse (53% in dev31, 11% since dev32;
#   53% again). The held-out half has no song it affects either way.
# * STRUCTURE_REPEAT_NEEDS_SUNG_TWICE (dev26) switched off: up on both halves.
# * STRUCTURE_REGRID_OCTAVE_FAST (off): the same for a grid at double tempo;
#   it did not help.
# ==============================================================================
# CHANGES IN 0.5-moss-dev35 (systematic test of the section namer; no change)
# ==============================================================================
# Section maps are exactly as in dev34 on all 148 saved scans. The namer was
# tested as a whole against the reference songs that are timed by ear (your
# timings and Harmonix), split into a half used while working and a half held
# out: every numeric setting tried at other values, every naming rule switched
# off in turn, and two new rules (see "dev35 settings"); nothing improved the
# held-out half, so nothing is switched on. The chorus "hook score" weights
# are now settings (same values as before).
# * STRUCTURE_PRECHORUS_BUILDUP (off): a verse's last segment that builds up to
#   the chorus (voice higher, drums busier) is a Pre-Chorus.
# * STRUCTURE_PRECHORUS_BUILDUP_CUTS (off): the same build-up also adds a
#   section line inside a verse.
# ==============================================================================
# CHANGES IN 0.5-moss-dev34 (lyric alignment experiments, switched off)
# ==============================================================================
# Section maps are exactly as in dev33 on all 148 saved scans. Two ways of
# using a file's own lyrics more fully were built and tested, and are left
# off because each moved some maps closer to their reference timings and
# others further (see "dev34 settings"):
# * STRUCTURE_TAG_FRAGMENTS: transcribed fragments of a lyric line matched to it.
# * STRUCTURE_TAG_FILL_MISSED: lyric lines Whisper missed placed between the
#   lines around them.
# The learned namer (dev31) was re-tested fairly with 20 hand-timed songs: it
# improved none and lowered two, so it still has no table.
# ==============================================================================
# CHANGES IN 0.5-moss-dev33 (Whisper's invented lines kept out of the map)
# ==============================================================================
# Replayed on all 148 saved scans: five maps change -- four of the nine new
# test scans (each closer to its reference timings) and one older scan with
# no reference timings; the 143 others are exactly as in dev32. The new
# settings follow the dev32 ones.
# * STRUCTURE_FOREIGN_CHUNKS: lyric lines Whisper invented in another language
#   -- fluent-looking Welsh over music it could not make out, Khmer strings,
#   Japanese credit lines -- are left out of the lines the section map reads
#   (found in 24 of the 148 scans). A song really sung in another language
#   keeps its lines.
# * STRUCTURE_SPLIT_STOCK_PHRASES: Whisper's stock video phrases ("We'll be
#   right back", "guitar solo", "Thanks for watching") split over two or three
#   lyric lines are left out too (found in 39 scans; the transcription filter
#   only caught them on one line).
# * STRUCTURE_TITLE_PICKS_ANY_WORDED: the dev32 title check weighs every part
#   that repeats its words; a rescan measured the pre-chorus at 1.00 and the
#   title chorus at 0.83, just outside the old margin, and undid the fix.
# * STRUCTURE_WORDLESS_CODA: after the last chorus, verse-named music heard
#   nowhere earlier and sung without words ("ooh", "yeah") is Instrumental.
# * STRUCTURE_ONEOFF_VERSE_SLOT_ANY: the one sung part between the first two
#   choruses is a verse, not a bridge, also when the song has a recurring
#   verse elsewhere.
# The lyrics text in the report is not changed by any of these.
# ==============================================================================
# CHANGES IN 0.5-moss-dev32 (the song's title as chorus evidence)
# ==============================================================================
# Replayed on all 139 saved scans: four maps change -- the two new test songs
# and two others, both closer to how they are sung; the 135 others are exactly
# as in dev31. Settings under "dev32 settings".
# * STRUCTURE_TITLE_PICKS_CHORUS: when a chorus and a pre-chorus repeat their
#   words about equally, the one that sings the title every time is the
#   chorus (the louder-sounding pre-chorus had been named Chorus).
# * STRUCTURE_PRECHORUS_AFTER_VERSE: a word-repeating part that always follows
#   the verse and first leads into the chorus is a Pre-Chorus, even when later
#   passes lead into a bridge or solo (it had been merged into the verse).
# * STRUCTURE_TITLE_SEGMENTS: in a song played on one riff throughout, where
#   neither the music nor repeated words separate verse from chorus, passages
#   that sing the title two or more times are Chorus and Chorus-named passages
#   that never sing it are Verse.
# ==============================================================================
# CHANGES IN 0.5-moss-dev31 (anonymous references, learned namer, title line)
# ==============================================================================
# Replayed on all 137 saved scans: one map changes (below); the 136 others are
# exactly as in dev30.
# * Comments no longer name test songs or quote their lyrics; the code itself
#   is unchanged by that (checked by comparing the parsed program).
# * STRUCTURE_TITLE_HOOK_FUZZY: the title-line check behind the verse/chorus
#   swap (STRUCTURE_TITLE_HOOK) also counts a three-word-or-longer title sung
#   with one word misheard, once per section. One dance-pop test song had its
#   verses and choruses swapped; it now matches its reference 93% (was 30%).
# * STRUCTURE_LEARNED_NAMER: reference songs can now check section names
#   directly. musiclyse_check.py --train-namer turns reference timings into a
#   table of anonymous numbers (musiclyse_namer.json, next to this script);
#   sections that look like reference sections named otherwise are renamed --
#   names only, never a section line. With no table file nothing changes, and
#   none is included: on the current references a fair test (each song named
#   by a table built without it) changed one song, for the worse, so it waits
#   for more references. --loso shows that test for your own table.
# ==============================================================================
# CHANGES IN 0.5-moss-dev30 (section names checked on 110 more saved scans)
# ==============================================================================
# Replayed on 137 saved scans (the 27 checked before, unchanged, and 110 dev25
# scans). 16 maps change; every one with reference timings is closer to them.
# Each rule has its own setting under "dev30 section-naming settings".
# * STRUCTURE_PRECHORUS_KEEPS_NAMES: a pre-chorus cut out by its words no
#   longer renames the rest of the song (the pre-chorus had been taken for the
#   chorus and the chorus called "Verse").
# * STRUCTURE_CHORUS_HALVES: a chorus of two unlike-sounding halves, both
#   repeating their words, always back to back and equally loud, is one
#   chorus (one half had been "Verse" or "Post-Chorus").
# * STRUCTURE_ONEOFF_VERSE_SLOT: the one sung part straight between the first
#   and second chorus is the second verse, not "Bridge".
# * STRUCTURE_SUNG_INTRO_TAG_OR_SPEECH: an opening that repeats one short
#   line, a quiet spoken sample before the band, or an opening the lyric sheet
#   marks [Intro] is the Intro again (dev26 had made these "Verse 1").
# * STRUCTURE_REPEATED_BRIDGE: a part heard after a chorus each time it comes,
#   with the same words, unlike the verse, is a Bridge (it had been "Verse" and
#   merged into a 98-second verse).
# * STRUCTURE_LONE_SUNG_IS_VERSE: the one sung passage of an otherwise
#   instrumental, non-repeating track is "Verse", not "Section".
# * STRUCTURE_KNOWN_PART_WORDS_VETO: the dev26 known-part split no longer
#   copies a misplaced section line from elsewhere in the song.
# ==============================================================================
# CHANGES IN 0.5-moss-dev29 (verse numbering; optional trailing silence)
# ==============================================================================
# * STRUCTURE_VERSE_HALVES_AS_PARTS: one verse kept in two halves is numbered
#   "Verse 1 Part 1 / Part 2", so the next verse is "Verse 2" (it was
#   "Verse 1 / Verse 2 / Verse 3"). Names only; times unchanged.
