# CHANGES IN 0.5-moss-dev18 (unbroken boys' voices; the listening model's key)
# ==============================================================================
# Ten tracks scanned with both 0.5 and dev17 were compared. The decided key was
# the same in both on all ten; the two problems were these.
# * YOUNG_VOICE_CONFIRM_FLIP: a boy or teenager (child/adolescent male
#   identity) whose voice the first vocal pass heard as a woman's, where only
#   the confirmation pass heard a man's and the measured median pitch (both
#   readings) is at or above VOCAL_DEEPENED_MAX_MEDIAN_HZ, gets the unbroken-
#   voice sentence in the SINGER IDENTITY note instead of "his voice may
#   already have been deepening". MOSS-Music's confirmation pass called
#   15-year-old the teen singer on the teen-pop test track (median E4, F#4 note by note)
#   "post_puberty_male ... a mature voice" against its own first pass's
#   female_teen_adult (95%); 0.5 kept the female reading, which the identity
#   step turns into his unbroken voice. Saved files get the new sentence when
#   loaded. Below that pitch (dev69's deepened voice), without a young male
#   identity, or when the first pass already heard a man, nothing changes.
# * KEY_NOTE_CHECK: MOSS-Music answers "Eb major", "Eb minor" or "Ab minor"
#   for the key on most tracks (six of ten here, whatever the song is in).
#   0.5's listening model agreed with Essentia on eight of the ten, so dev17
#   printed "methods disagreed" beside correct keys, and wherever Essentia's
#   strength is under 0.6 reconcile_key took the stock answer. After the stems
#   are transcribed, both keys are scored against the notes of every pitched
#   part (duration-weighted, Krumhansl-Kessler profiles). When the notes fit
#   Essentia's key (0.6 or more, within 0.1 of the best of the 24 keys) and not
#   the listening model's (under 0.5, 0.3 or more behind), the RECOMMENDED KEY
#   block says so: next to a strong Essentia key it is a clear reading, and in
#   place of a stock answer that had won over a weak Essentia key, Essentia's
#   key is used. On the fourteen saved scans tested it rewords the block on
#   eight and changes no key; the stock answers fit at -0.65 to 0.07, Essentia's
#   keys at 0.73 to 0.96. A listening-model key the notes do fit (Eb major for
#   a song Essentia reads as C minor) is left alone. /load applies it to files
#   saved before dev18.
# * Not changed: KEY_CENTRE_CHECK (it fires on none of these scans) and the
#   modal-inflection lines. The tempo has the same weakness as the key --
#   MOSS-Music's own figure (127 BPM on the teen-pop test track, where 0.5 had 144 and the
#   song is about 146) wins where the detectors disagree -- and is left for a
#   later dev.
#
# ==============================================================================
# CHANGES IN 0.5 (MOSS-Music-8B-Instruct is the listening model)
# ==============================================================================
# * The listening model -- the model that hears the track for the era pass,
#   the main field report, the vocal and confirmation passes, the voice
#   description, the section-name hints and the self-check -- is now
#   MOSS-Music-8B-Instruct (OpenMOSS-Team/MOSS-Music-8B-Instruct, Qwen3-8B with
#   a MOSS audio encoder). It replaces the previous listening model
#   everywhere; flags, functions and notes are renamed MOSS_* / moss_* (e.g.
#   MOSS_LOOP_GUARD, MOSS_CONSISTENT_REPORT, get_moss_music()). The flag that
#   skips the Whisper lyrics pass when the file has lyric tags is now
#   SKIP_LYRICS_TRANSCRIPTION_WHEN_TAGS_PRESENT.
# * Loading: MOSS-Music's model/processor code is imported from a clone of
#   OpenMOSS's GitHub repo (MOSS_MUSIC_CODE_DIR, default ~/MOSS-Music), as
#   OpenMOSS's own infer.py does; the weights and tokenizer come from
#   Hugging Face (MOSS_MODEL_REVISION can pin a commit). The model repo's own
#   copy of the code is not used: its config leaves the audio-encoder
#   settings as a plain dict and the model fails to build ('dict' object has
#   no attribute 'downsample_hidden_size'), and loading through it makes
#   transformers ask "Do you wish to run the custom code?". Its processor's 2-second time
#   markers are switched on (MOSS_ENABLE_TIME_MARKERS), which its
#   from_pretrained() otherwise leaves off. Its code targets transformers
#   4.57; _moss_transformers_compat() adapts it at load time to transformers
#   5.x (Whisper encoder layers now return a tensor; generate() no longer
#   passes cache_position), so the transformers already installed for the
#   previous model keeps working. It also scores only the last prompt
#   position, as built-in transformers models do, which avoids a 3-6 GB
#   spike per pass.
# * Prompting: MOSS-Music's processor takes one ChatML prompt plus audio
#   arrays rather than a chat template, so moss_generate() turns the same
#   conversations into that prompt (system / user / assistant turns, audio
#   first in its turn). System-message retries, the field-format prefill, the
#   self-check's two-turn conversation and per-track follow-up history work as
#   before.
# * Audio: still the 16 kHz mono copy of the track (decoded once per track and
#   reused across passes), cut at MOSS_MAX_AUDIO_SECONDS = 600 -- the same
#   10-minute window the previous model had. URLs are fetched with ffmpeg.
#   Nothing else changes source: the stereo/loudness/width measurements and
#   Demucs (whose stems feed Omnizart) still read the original file, and
#   Essentia, PANNs and the beat/timbre report still read their 22.05 kHz copy.
# * Audio position table: on transformers 5.x, MOSS-Music's audio encoder
#   loaded with its sinusoidal position table uninitialised (zeros or random
#   memory), so the model could not tell where in the track each audio frame
#   was -- the cause of invented era evidence, runaway loops and empty report
#   fields in the first test scans. _moss_transformers_compat() recomputes
#   it; output on 5.15 and 5.20 is now token-identical to transformers 4.57.
# * Decoding: MOSS-Music now samples (MOSS_DECODING = "sampled";
#   T=0.7, top_p=0.8, top_k=20, as Qwen3 recommends) instead of always taking
#   the likeliest word, which made it loop and drop fields. Sampling runs on
#   the CPU from a fixed seed (MOSS_SEED): PyTorch's sampling on Apple GPUs
#   returned stray tokens, and the seed keeps scans repeatable on one machine.
# * Guided field names (MOSS_GUIDED_FIELDS): for the main report, self-check,
#   recovery, era, vocal and confirmation passes the script writes each
#   field name itself, in the template's order, and MOSS-Music writes only
#   the values; each value is capped (MOSS_FIELD_MAX_TOKENS, and a fair share
#   of the remaining budget) so every field is reached. On a duo test track the
#   unguided greedy main report filled 6 of 18 fields and the vocal pass
#   looped on "NUM_DETECTED_DETECTED...".
# * Memory: the Python process sat at ~73 GB (memory pressure yellow) while
#   MOSS-Music generated on a 64 GB Mac, because PyTorch's Apple-GPU
#   allocator only reclaims cached blocks above its LOW watermark (default
#   ~67 GB there). PYTORCH_MPS_LOW_WATERMARK_RATIO is now 0.6 (~29 GB), and
#   the cache is also released every 256 tokens and after each pass
#   (MOSS_MPS_CACHE_TRIM_EVERY).
# * Short era / vocal / confirmation prompts (MOSS_SHORT_PROMPTS): the same
#   questions and the same output fields, without the long rule lists. With
#   the long prompts MOSS-Music rated a guest singer (31, high rock tenor) as a
#   child and then an adolescent, and dated a duo test track (1978) "2010s-2020s,
#   high confidence" from "modern loudness". The short vocal prompts judge
#   age from vocal weight and resonance rather than pitch, and say a sung
#   median of 250-350 Hz fits a high adult male lead or a woman singing low;
#   the short era prompt leaves loudness and polish out unless measurements
#   are supplied, since remasters make old records loud. VOCALS_PRESENT is
#   "yes" if anyone sings anywhere in the track (main report too). False
#   restores the long prompts (kept as *_PROMPT_LONG).
# * DESCRIPTION: the main report and self-check now end with a DESCRIPTION
#   field. With guided field names the reply stopped after the last field,
#   so MOSS-Music wrote no free description, the report's DESCRIPTION was
#   "(none given)", and fields it left uncertain could not be filled from
#   its description as they were with the previous model.
# * Age guard input: _apply_vocal_age_guard no longer reads the vocal pass's
#   PROBABILITY_ESTIMATE line (or a FORMANT_NOTE that copies its option list)
#   as voice-change evidence. That line repeats the template's own category
#   names ("adolescent male (voice changing/recently changed)", "female
#   teen/adult") whatever the numbers, so every reply that filled it in
#   demoted an adult male lead to adolescent_male_likely. The previous model
#   wrote "not available" there, so it never came up. The guard's rule is
#   unchanged. "adult-sized" (the template's adult FORMANT_NOTE answer) now
#   vetoes a demotion of an adult male the way "adolescent-transitional"
#   already counted towards one (A1's lead was demoted for "youthful timbre"
#   next to "adult-sized resonance"); it is not used to decide a child's
#   gender, and an explicit "voice still changing" still demotes.
# * Copied answers (MOSS_NO_FIELD_COPYING): on three scans in eighteen the
#   main report pasted one answer into every remaining field ("none
#   (0.00s-13.92s)" on the post-punk test track, "none (static harmony, no tonal
#   movement)" on another test track). The vocal-presence check read that as "no vocals"
#   and declared both songs instrumental, dropping their lyrics, singer
#   analysis and section names (all "Section" / "Theme A"). The decoder now
#   rules out the word that would continue such a copy; a copied run in a
#   report is treated as not given (the first pass's own values are used,
#   or the measured ones); and a VOCALS_PRESENT answer limited to a stretch
#   of time ("none (0.00s-13.92s)") is not taken as a whole-track claim.
# * Drum bleed (DRUM_BLEED_NOTE): when the separated drums stem sits 18 dB or
#   more below all stems combined (1-10 dB on every band recording tested,
#   21 and 54 dB on the two acoustic ones), its transcribed "hits" are
#   guitar/hand bleed: the drum report, grooves, beat grids, timeline and
#   per-section drum intensity are replaced by a DRUM STEM NOTE saying
#   there is no kit, and the writer is told the note overrides any drum
#   mention, as the LOW-END NOTE already did for bass.
# * Report vocals: a VOCALS_PRESENT that is neither yes nor no ("uncertain
#   (vocal texture present but lyrics unclear)", "mostly yes") is replaced by
#   the measured answer, including the dedicated vocal pass's own yes when
#   no instrumental verdict overruled it; with a voice settled as present, a
#   LEAD_VOCAL_CHARACTERISTICS of "none (no lead vocal)" is not kept.
# * Melody: MELODIC_RANGE and MELODIC_CONTOUR are the measured vocal range and
#   per-section vocal contour whenever those exist, as in every scan the
#   previous listening model made. MOSS-Music filled both, mostly with the
#   prompt's own examples ("C4-G5", "stepwise ascending in verse, leap up in
#   chorus"); the examples are gone from the prompt. When the pitch tracker's
#   reading is marked LOW confidence, the range comes from the vocal stem's
#   note reading, and a LOW-confidence tracker median three or more semitones
#   from the note reading is replaced by it in the VOCAL PITCH REPORT and its
#   voice-type map (_pitch_low_confidence_check; the post-punk test track's 622 Hz
#   "falsetto / countertenor" reading becomes the notes' A#3). The age/gender
#   guards still use the measured median as before.
# * Vocal-presence check (VOCAL_ARBITRATION_LOUD_STEM_GUARD): the main pass's
#   "instrumental" no longer decides on its own, with a low pitched-frame
#   share, when the dedicated vocal pass hears a voice and the vocal stem is
#   within 12 dB of the mix. Every sung track tested sits 2-9 dB below the
#   mix while being confidently pitched in as little as 0.2-6% of frames;
#   MOSS-Music's main pass called a duo test track instrumental ("Despite the
#   absence of vocals") while its own melody notes described the vocal line.
#   A quiet stem, or both listening passes hearing no voice, still rules a
#   track instrumental.
# * Report KEY / TEMPO_BPM: the decided key and tempo (RECOMMENDED KEY / TEMPO
#   FOR DISCUSSION) where the model's own value disagrees. MOSS-Music wrote
#   "Eb major" / "Ab minor (confidence: high)" on most tracks, or the
#   prompt's instruction text, and DESCRIPTION repeated it; the existing
#   description tidy now drops those sentences. With a DRUM STEM NOTE, the
#   report's DRUMS says none and drum items leave INSTRUMENTATION.
# * With a voice settled as present, the model's own "absence of vocals"
#   sentences are kept out of LEAD_VOCAL_CHARACTERISTICS and DESCRIPTION, and
#   an empty or no-voice LEAD_VOCAL_CHARACTERISTICS is filled from the
#   dedicated vocal pass's own description (LEAD_PROFILE_NOTE / TIMBRE_NOTE /
#   DELIVERY_NOTE). a duo test track's report had copied "Despite the absence of
#   vocals in this recording ..." into that field.
# * Key centre check (KEY_CENTRE_CHECK): Essentia picks only between major
#   and minor keys, so a song in D with a C chord (D Mixolydian, the notes of
#   G major) was reported as G major. After the stems are transcribed, a
#   major key is reported as Mixolydian on its fifth only when the low part
#   and the vocal line both centre on the fifth (1.5x the old root or more),
#   the chord parts favour it, and the fifth's flat 7th is played with no
#   leading tone. The RECOMMENDED KEY block then reads e.g. "D Mixolydian (the
#   notes of G major, centred on D)" with the evidence, the melody blocks
#   count scale degrees from D, and the existing modal-inflection test
#   confirms it independently. Of the 35 saved scans tested it changes only
#   the amplified-folk test track (whose writer says D); every other key is left exactly as it was.
# * "Nothing detected" answers: three or more report fields saying the model
#   heard nothing ("none (no audio detected)", "none (no instruments
#   detected)") count as unanswered, like a copied run. Blocked from pasting
#   one answer everywhere, MOSS-Music wrote that in varied words across eight
#   fields on a folk test track. A single honest "DRUMS=none (no drums)" is kept.
# * Era drum evidence: with a DRUM STEM NOTE, an "ERA NOTE (drums)" line marks
#   the era pass's drum observations as contradicted ("vintage drum
#   machines" on a folk test track, "analog-style drum recording" on a folk-rock test track).
# * Synth denial (ARRANGEMENT CHECK): when the listening model says there are
#   no synths or keyboards while the 'other' stem carries a real chordal part
#   (3+ notes at once, 300+ notes) that peaks somewhere and plays in a third
#   of the sections, an ARRANGEMENT CHECK names where it peaks and says not to
#   deny it; the tagger's weak organ / strings / brass labels for that stem are
#   called what they usually are, readings of a bright synth or keyboard. The
#   existing check needed the part to outrank the guitars over the whole
#   track, which a duo test track's saw-wave synth (peaking in the instrumental)
#   never does. Applied when a track is loaded, so saved scans get it too.
#   It stands down when the tagger confirms (whole mix agrees) strings, horns,
#   woodwinds, harmonica, accordion or choir on that stem, since a real section
#   can carry such a part with no synth. Fires on the two a duo test track scans
#   only; the other 38 saved scans' writer views are byte-identical.
# * Reed tags: a strong, steady 'harmonica' reading (75%+ in 75%+ of windows,
#   not stem-only) is kept as a harmonica-like or brassy lead instead of being
#   dismissed (a pop-rock test track's main lead, which the listening model missed); an
#   accordion tag on an 'other' stem playing 4+ note chords is called most
#   likely a synth pad (a pop test track, the teen-pop test track, two rock-ballad test tracks).
#   A stored INSTRUMENT CHECK in a saved scan is refreshed only when one of
#   these readings applies. See INSTRUMENT_REED_STRONG_LEAD.
# * Beat-drawing questions phrased "draw out the beats", "show me the rhythm",
#   "map out the chorus groove" now count as grid questions, so a reply that
#   wrongly says there is no beat grid is held back and corrected before it is
