#   chorus is linked to the others by its words (STRUCTURE_LYRIC_PHRASES).
# * Tracks whose files carry their lyrics skipped Whisper, so their section
#   map had no lyric timing at all. One Whisper decode now times the tag
#   lyrics for the section map only; the report still uses the tag lyrics
#   (STRUCTURE_TIME_TAG_LYRICS; costs one Whisper pass on those tracks).
# * Instrumental stretches: 4+ bars with no voice at the start or end of a
#   verse/chorus (a riff, a solo, a break) become their own Instrumental
#   section (or join the Intro/Outro), and a part with the verse's or
#   chorus's music but no voice is Instrumental (STRUCTURE_SPLIT_INSTRUMENTAL).
# * Blind questions ("without knowing the artist or year, when does this sound
#   like it's from?", "ignore the metadata", "based only on the sound") are
#   answered from a separate copy of the evidence with the artist, title,
#   album, year, label, lyrics, background articles, singer identity and cover
#   art removed and tracks renamed Track 1, 2... Earlier replies, which may
#   name the artist, are left out of that one request (BLIND_QUESTIONS).
#   "Blind test: guess the band" stays blind and lets the writer guess;
#   a question asking for the lyrics is answered normally.
# * The listening model: a runaway repeating line (the rock track's 271-chord list)
#   is ended so the report reaches its remaining fields (MOSS_LOOP_GUARD). A
#   report that does not loop is produced exactly as before; the first-pass
#   prompt is unchanged for every track.
# Verified: every existing structure test, the perturbation and noisy-lyrics
# stability tests and all report-text checks are identical to dev37.
#
# ==============================================================================
# CHANGES IN 0.4-dev37 (fixes from an external code review)
# ==============================================================================
# None of these touch the analysis itself; section maps, reports and saved
# JSON content are unchanged.
# * Ollama fallback contexts: the retry ladder steps down to num_ctx 16384 and
#   8192 but still asked for a 16384-token reply, leaving no room for the
#   prompt (and batch cover-art / singer-identity calls run at 8192 with the
#   same 16384 request). The reply budget is now at most half the window at
#   each attempt; unchanged at 32768 and above, i.e. for every normal
#   interactive request.
# * /batch name collisions: two files with the same Artist/Title tags shared
#   "Artist - Title.json", so the second was skipped as "already saved" (or,
#   with skipping off, overwrote the first). A save made from a different
#   source file is now left alone and the new one is saved as
#   "Artist - Title (2).json". Re-running /batch still skips every file that
#   already has its own save -- including one whose folder was moved or
#   copied, or that was renamed since -- and saves from older versions (which
#   record no source) are treated exactly as before. A save only counts as
#   another file's when that file still exists and is a different recording.
# * Help text: /clearall keeps saved-songs/ files (it always did; the help
#   said it wiped them), and the setup line names the exact default model
#   (ollama pull muse-glimmer:30b-mlx). A startup warning now says when
#   OLLAMA_MODEL is not installed, with the pull command to run.
# * Streamed answers: when the beat-grid cross-check could ask the writer to
#   answer again (a grid / drum-pattern question naming a loaded track that
#   has beat grids; judged from the person's own words, not the blocks the
#   pipeline adds around them), that reply is generated in full before it is shown,
#   so a wrong first answer is never printed above the corrected one. All
#   other replies still stream live.
# * Ollama "is the writer loaded?" check: with a tagged model name, another
#   tag of the same model (muse-glimmer:30b vs :30b-mlx) no longer counts, so
#   unloading no longer loads the configured model just to drop it again.
#   Untagged names keep the old any-tag behaviour.
#
# ==============================================================================
# CHANGES IN 0.4-dev36 (instrumentation / dynamics / report-wording accuracy)
# ==============================================================================
# Checked against independent measurements of the two test tracks (ffmpeg
# EBU R128, pyloudnorm, 4x-oversampled true peak, spectrum): LUFS, LRA, true
# peak, PLR, crest factor and stereo width were all already correct. Fixed:
# * The listening model's chord list: the runaway-loop collapser never fired on
#   the rock track -- it did not split on en dashes, its block pattern could
#   not match at the end of the text, and the loop's last chord was cut off
#   mid-name. A second, line-only pass now collapses a chord line only when
#   it is a runaway loop (32+ chords, the loop repeated 6+ times, period 1-6
#   matching at 95%+ of positions); real progression lists, 12-bar blues and
#   song-length lists with a bridge are left as they are, nothing past the
#   chords line can be touched, and fields after it on the same line are
#   kept. dev35's own collapse rule is unchanged.
# * When dev35's system-message retry rescues an empty main pass but the
#   report stops early (the rock track: genre, key, tempo, then the chord
#   loop), one free-form description pass is added so instrumentation,
#   timbre, mood and vocals are not lost (MOSS_EMPTY_RETRY_ADD_DESCRIPTION).
#   Any genre / key / tempo / chords line it writes that the main report
#   already has (in any spelling, at a line start or inline) is dropped, so
#   it can only add, never override.
# * "VOCALS_PRESENT" fallback: "an instrumental intro / break / solo /
#   backing" (and plurals) no longer reads as "the track has no vocals" --
#   both test tracks' free-form passes say "an instrumental intro
#   establishes...". "An instrumental track" still reads as no vocals.
# * Guitar stem: a note count alone no longer makes it "carrying a real
#   part" -- an AI-generated test track's guitar stem held 149 residue notes at -53 dBFS RMS,
#   quieter than its piano stem, which was already called silent. The
#   inconclusive-guitar note no longer suggests "a bright, overdriven
#   midrange rhythm layer" as the default wording.
# * Instrument presence by section keeps the "weak: this stem only" caveat
#   (an AI-generated test track's bass stem read "bass guitar / guitar / plucked strings
#   throughout" with nothing to say none was confirmed).
# * Crest factor: one set of band boundaries, so 14.0 dB is not "high crest"
#   on one line and "moderate" on the next.
# * Bandwidth provenance by file type: an MP3 is called a lossy encode
#   outright; a WAV/FLAC/AIFF with a sharp shelf is no longer said to have
#   "very probably been through lossy compression" -- the limit came from
#   upstream (a lossy encode, a band-limited source, or resampling). Files
#   of unknown type (.m4a, which may be AAC or ALAC) read as before.
#
# ==============================================================================
# CHANGES IN 0.4-dev35 (vocal-tag regression fix, empty main pass)
# ==============================================================================
# * Regression fix: dev34 let the vocal-tag parser read "LEAD PROFILE" (with a
#   space). an AI-generated test track's vocal pass then wrote a free-text heading, "LEAD
#   PROFILE: gender uncertain", and an old alias mapped "gender_uncertain" to
#   child_gender_uncertain -- so an adult-sounding singer became a CHILD lead,
#   which also triggered a confirmation pass that agreed. The parser is back
#   to the underscore tags only, and the alias is removed ("gender
#   uncertain" says nothing about age; LEAD_CATEGORY decides instead).
# * Looping vocal passes: the copy written with the proper underscore tags is
#   the one kept, and long lines count as repeats when they differ by a
#   reworded word (the rock track's vocal pass looped three times with
#   "proxies" -> "prefixes" in one line).
# * Listening-model main pass: when it comes back empty, the same full
#   instructions are first retried as a system message with a short request
#   beside the audio (MOSS_EMPTY_RETRY_SYSTEM_PROMPT), before the short recovery
#   prompt. The rock
#   track's main pass has failed identically in every version since dev31,
#   and the recovery prompt returns a free-form caption without the
#   GENRE_RANKED / KEY / TEMPO_BPM / CHORDS / STRUCTURE fields. Tracks whose
#   main pass works are unaffected.
#
# ==============================================================================
# CHANGES IN 0.4-dev34 (section-map stability pass)
# ==============================================================================
# Demucs/Omnizart give slightly different notes on every run (a few hits more
# or fewer per stem), and on the dev33 re-run that alone moved the rock
# track's Chorus 2 to 2:06 and an AI-generated test track's Chorus 3 to 2:17, one bar late each.
# Tested here by re-running the section map on 12 randomly perturbed copies of
# each run's notes (3% dropped, 2% added, 10 ms jitter).
# * Drum fills: one fill now scores one bar line -- the one it leads into --
#   instead of two neighbouring ones with near-equal scores; the score ramps
#   up from 80% of the threshold rather than switching on at one exact snare
#   count; and a boundary placed at the START of a fill bar moves to the bar
#   line the fill leads into.
# * New evidence: the kick returning after two or more kick-less bars (the
#   breakdown-to-chorus return), weighted like a drum entry and counted as a
#   strong split between back-to-back recurring sections.
# * The 4-bar phrase preference is now a setting, STRUCTURE_PHRASE_PENALTY
#   (default unchanged at 0.15; lower values were tested and traded one
#   an AI-generated test track boundary for another).
# * Result: rock is unchanged under every perturbation (dev33: Chorus 2
#   moved in 9 of 24 runs); an AI-generated test track F@3 0.94/0.84 -> 0.99/0.94 on the two
#   runs' notes (its verse-to-chorus change at 0:37 is weak in the audio
#   alone, and still slips in 2 of 12 perturbed runs without lyrics).
#   Synthetic suite and noisy-lyrics test unchanged.
# * The vocal-tag parser accepted "LEAD CATEGORY" as well as "LEAD_CATEGORY"
#   (reverted in dev35 -- it let a free-text heading through).
# * A looping vocal pass whose copies differ slightly ("LEAD_CATEGORY" vs
#   "LEAD CATEGORY") or end in a cut-off copy is now collapsed too.
#
# ==============================================================================
# CHANGES IN 0.4-dev33 (groove, vocal, genre and wording accuracy pass)
# ==============================================================================
# * Drum groove: grid coverage and grid-lock are now also measured window by
#   window (_local_cell_coverage / _local_circular_lock), so timing drift or
#   small jumps (live takes, AI-generated audio) no longer turn a steady
#   pattern into "syncopated". an AI-generated test track's kick (one every 0.500 s) had been
#   reported as "NOT four-on-the-floor".
# * Snare: coverage-led classification (eighth / every beat / every other
#   beat / once per bar) runs before the hit-rate tests, so fills and ghost
#   notes cannot hide the pattern. New "half-time backbeat" class (snare on
#   beat 3), checked against the kick so a 2-and-4 backbeat with missed
#   snares is not called half-time; an every-other-beat snare that clearly
#   lands between the kicks is no longer called a backbeat. Beat-grid
#   diagrams put a half-time snare on 3.
# * Kick "half-time kick (1 & 3)" renamed "kick on every other beat" -- that
#   is a standard rock kick, not a half-time feel. Prompt wording updated.
# * Vocals: the f0-only upgrade to "mixed leads / duet" now needs 7.5
#   semitones between pitch centres (was 6.0, which a single singer's
#   verse-to-chorus lift reaches). Voice-type map recalibrated for sung
#   pop/rock medians (C4-E4 is ordinary tenor territory, not falsetto); male
#   f0 caps raised to 350 Hz so pitch alone no longer calls an adult rock
#   tenor "adolescent"; female-mislabel recovery keeps its 320 Hz threshold.
# * Genre: when PANNs points at another family and the listening model's own ranked list
#   names a genre in that family (e.g. "dance-pop"), that label is used.
#   Unicode hyphens in genre labels are normalised.
# * Wording: tempo notes print one decimal; no "..", and the tempo example
#   uses the actual tempo; the full-resolution crest factor says why it
#   differs from the analysis-copy figure; very fast tracker pulses explain
#   their bar length; a wrong track length in the listening model's prose is corrected; a
#   lyric passage the second transcription skipped is called unconfirmed,
#   not "disagreeing"; the raw novelty boundary list is labelled a
#   cross-check.
#
# ==============================================================================
# CHANGES IN 0.4-dev32 (song-structure accuracy pass)
# ==============================================================================
# * Song structure is rebuilt ("SONG STRUCTURE v2", build_structure_map):
#   bar-level boundary evidence from the mix (timbre/loudness novelty, where
#   repeated chord sequences start and stop) plus arrangement events from the
#   separated stems (drum fills, drum and vocal entries, vocal register
#   changes) and lyric timing, chosen by a dynamic programme with a
#   section-length prior. A boundary at the start of a short break moves to
#   where the band comes back in. Digital silence at either end becomes a
#   "Silence" section.
# * Section names come from measured evidence instead of a template: the
#   chorus is the recurring sung material with the most hook evidence
#   (repeated words, higher vocal register, energy) wherever it first occurs,
#   so chorus-first songs are no longer named verse-first. Repetition is
#   counted in runs, so a section split in two is not mistaken for a repeat.
#   Instrumentals use Theme A/B; through-composed material stays "Section".
# * The final map is computed after stems and lyrics exist and is primary;
#   the listening model's own STRUCTURE timestamps are only a last resort.
#   Its names are still used for unsung parts (solo, breakdown...).
# * Beat grid: tempo refined below librosa's whole-frame resolution (120 BPM
#   was read as 117.45, ~158 as 161.5), beats tracked on a kick-anchored
#   envelope at a hop fine enough for that tempo, and bar lines chosen by a
#   Viterbi pass that survives a beat the tracker inserts or drops.
# * Timestamps display like a player's elapsed counter (24.6 s -> 0:24).
# * RECOMMENDED KEY no longer carries decoration such as "(as supplied)".
# * Non-stylistic AudioSet tags (e.g. "Christian") no longer steer the
#   recommended genre; genre families are compared by total classifier mass.
# * The objective tempo line reports the refined tempo and beat spacing.
# * STRUCTURE fields written by this script ("Name | M:SS-M:SS") parse back.
# Set STRUCTURE_V2_ENABLED = False (USER CONFIGURATION) to restore the
# previous structure pipeline.
# ==============================================================================

