# --- Section-labelling thresholds (naming only, never timestamps) -----------
# These decide what a measured segment is CALLED once its boundaries are fixed.
# They do not move any boundary. Previously inline magic numbers; named here so
# labelling behaviour can be tuned without editing the labelling logic.
#
# A first segment longer than this is treated as a real opening section (verse,
# chorus, whatever the recurrence says) rather than an intro.
# (STRUCTURE_INTRO_MAX_S moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)
# Likewise for a closing segment and "Outro".
# (STRUCTURE_OUTRO_MAX_S moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)
# A one-off segment is only called a Bridge when it sits in this fractional
# window of the track. The upper bound deliberately excludes the FINAL segment:
# a closing section is not a bridge. Widening it toward 1.0 will start calling
# closing sections bridges.
# (STRUCTURE_BRIDGE_MIN_POS moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)
# (STRUCTURE_BRIDGE_MAX_POS moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)

# --- Instrumental-section detection ----------------------------------------
# A section is called instrumental when transcribed vocal-stem notes cover less
# than this fraction of its length. Measured separation is wide: on a sung pop
# track, vocal sections cover 68-84% of their span while a genuine instrumental
# intro/outro covers 0-5%, so this sits in the middle of a very large gap and
# tolerates imperfect vocal transcription in both directions.
# (STRUCTURE_INSTRUMENTAL_MAX_COVERAGE moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)
# Instrumental labelling is only applied to tracks that HAVE singing somewhere:
# at least one section must reach this coverage. Without the guard, a fully
# instrumental piece would have every one of its sections labelled
# "Instrumental", which is noise rather than information — such a track keeps
# neutral Section names instead.
# (STRUCTURE_VOCAL_TRACK_MIN_COVERAGE moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)
# Trim verbose lists from the independent DSP report (downbeats, band-onset table).
# (COMPACT_OBJECTIVE_REPORT moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)
# When file-tag lyrics exist, skip appending the dedicated Whisper lyrics transcription.
# The tag lyrics are authoritative for lyric questions and should not be mixed with
# snippet-level ASR output. Name kept for configuration compatibility.
# (SKIP_LYRICS_TRANSCRIPTION_WHEN_TAGS_PRESENT moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)
# (DEMUCS_MODEL moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)

# --- Per-stem instrument tagging (optional) ---------------------------------
# The stem/MIDI stack (Demucs + Omnizart) only transcribes PITCH -- it has no
# concept of timbre/instrument identity. Before this, instrument identity was
# left entirely to the writer LLM's own listening + coarse whole-mix spectral
# stats (brightness/rolloff/flatness), which is weak for e.g. telling synth
# vs. electric guitar vs. strings apart inside the catch-all "other" stem.
# This adds a genuine independent signal: a pretrained AudioSet tagger run on
# each stem, the same role Essentia plays for tempo/key. Optional dependency
# (pip install panns-inference); if it's not installed, tagging is silently
# skipped and everything else behaves exactly as before.
# (ENABLE_INSTRUMENT_TAGGING moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)
# Raised from 0.08: AudioSet guitar/synth/strings classes often fire weakly on
# bright or midrange content that is not actually that instrument. Prefer
# omission over weak positives — the writer still has the listening model + stems.
# (INSTRUMENT_TAG_MIN_PROB moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)
# (INSTRUMENT_TAG_TOP_K moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)
# Guitar-family labels are especially noisy on synths, distorted bass, and
# residual bleed. Require a higher bar (and preferably whole-mix agreement).
# (INSTRUMENT_TAG_GUITAR_MIN_PROB moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)
INSTRUMENT_TAG_GUITAR_LABELS = (
    "electric guitar",
    "acoustic guitar",
    "guitar (type uncertain)",
    "slide/steel guitar",
)
# Which stems benefit from tagging. Vocals are already unambiguous once the
# vocal-stem detector fires. The big payoff is on the pitched/harmonic
# catch-all stems where identity is otherwise just a guess -- and on drums,
# which was previously excluded on the grounds that Omnizart's drum model
# already classifies it. That model only resolves kick, snare and hi-hat, so
# excluding drums meant nothing in the pipeline could ever report a crash, a
# ride, a tambourine or a shaker, no matter how prominent. The tagger's label
# map covers cymbal, tambourine, timpani, drum machine and drum roll, so it
# supplies exactly the kit detail the transcription cannot.
# (INSTRUMENT_TAG_STEMS moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)
# Drop a stem instrument tag when that stem has almost no pitched MIDI activity
# (Demucs residual / noise) unless the tag is very strong. Stops phantom
# "guitar on guitar stem" when the stem is effectively empty.
# (INSTRUMENT_TAG_REQUIRE_STEM_ACTIVITY moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)
# (INSTRUMENT_TAG_MIN_NOTES_FOR_WEAK moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)
# (INSTRUMENT_TAG_STRONG_PROB moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)

# Windowed tagging: instead of one prediction averaged over the whole clip
# (which dilutes anything that isn't present for most of the track, e.g. a
# guitar solo that only lasts 15s of a 3-minute song), the tagger is run on
# short overlapping windows and results are combined by taking, per label,
# the strongest window rather than a track-wide average. Set
# INSTRUMENT_TAG_WINDOW_SECONDS to None to fall back to old single-pass
# whole-clip behaviour.
# (INSTRUMENT_TAG_WINDOW_SECONDS moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)
# (INSTRUMENT_TAG_HOP_SECONDS moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)
# Cap on number of windows per clip, mainly to bound runtime on long tracks.
# Raised from 40: whole-mix instrument/genre/mood passes now share one cached
# set of inferences instead of re-running three times, so a larger budget
# costs less wall-clock than the old 40 did, and more windows means finer
# temporal resolution plus more reliable persistence counts.
# (INSTRUMENT_TAG_MAX_WINDOWS moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)
# Peak-normalize each window before tagging. PANNs was trained on full
# commercial/YouTube mixes; isolated Demucs stems (especially quieter ones
# like "other"/"piano") often sit well below that loudness, which silently
# suppresses genuinely-present instruments below INSTRUMENT_TAG_MIN_PROB.
# (INSTRUMENT_TAG_NORMALIZE moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)
# (INSTRUMENT_TAG_NORMALIZE_PEAK moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)

# --- Window coverage, persistence gating, and inference reuse ---------------
# COVERAGE FIX: windows used to be laid out strictly left-to-right from t=0 at
# a fixed hop until INSTRUMENT_TAG_MAX_WINDOWS was hit, which meant anything
# past ~(MAX_WINDOWS * HOP) seconds was never looked at at all -- on a 6-minute
# track the final 43% of the song was invisible to the tagger, so a string
# section that only enters in the last chorus, or an outro guitar solo, simply
# could not be detected. When the hop-spaced layout would exceed the window
# budget, starts are now distributed evenly across the WHOLE file instead of
# truncating at the start, so coverage is always end-to-end.
# (INSTRUMENT_TAG_EVEN_COVERAGE moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)

# PERSISTENCE GATING: results were combined by taking, per label, the single
# strongest window. That is the right call for detecting brief events (a 15s
# solo), but it also means one fluke window scores identically to an
# instrument audible throughout -- a classic false-positive source, because
# over 40 windows the chance that *some* window spuriously spikes is high.
# The tagger now also records in how many windows each label actually fired,
# so a one-window-only claim can be held to a higher bar while a sustained
# one is trusted at the normal bar. Set INSTRUMENT_TAG_MIN_WINDOW_HITS = 1 to
# restore the previous peak-only behaviour.
# (INSTRUMENT_TAG_MIN_WINDOW_HITS moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)
# A label seen in only one window must be this strong to survive.
# (INSTRUMENT_TAG_SINGLE_WINDOW_STRONG_PROB moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)
# Fraction of windows a label must fire in to count as "sustained" (reported
# to the writer, and allowed to pass mix-disagreement on its own merits).
# (INSTRUMENT_TAG_SUSTAINED_COVERAGE moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)
# Sustained-but-moderate tags are real instruments the peak-only rule used to
# under-rate; a label present in at least this share of windows is accepted
# slightly below its family threshold.
# (INSTRUMENT_TAG_SUSTAINED_PROB_RELIEF moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)

# PANNs inference over one file used to be repeated once per label map --
# whole-mix instruments, genre and mood each re-decoded the same audio and
# re-ran the same Cnn14 forward passes over the same windows, three times.
# Raw per-window probabilities are now cached and re-used across label maps,
# which makes the extra passes essentially free and buys back enough runtime
# to afford finer temporal resolution below.
# (PANNS_WINDOW_CACHE_SIZE moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)
# Persistence STATS (how many windows each label fired in) used to be evicted
# together with the probability matrices above, which is wrong: the memory
# pressure the LRU exists for is entirely in the (n_windows x 527) float32
# matrices, while the stats are a handful of floats per label. With a cache of
# 3 the whole-mix entry was always popped by the time the four per-stem passes
# had run, so the whole-mix tag report silently lost its window counts -- the
# sustained/intermittent/isolated distinction the writer prompt leans on -- and
# any later lookup on the mix had to re-run inference. Stats are now bounded
# separately and generously.
# (PANNS_TAG_STATS_CACHE_SIZE moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)

# --- Whole-mix instrument tagging (optional, in addition to per-stem) ------
# Per-stem tagging inherits whatever mistakes Demucs made when separating
# (e.g. a synth pad bleeding into "other" alongside real strings). Tagging
# the original, un-separated mix is a second, independent source of truth
# that isn't subject to separation artifacts, and can help flag cases where
# a stem's tags look suspicious relative to what's audible in the full mix.
# (ENABLE_WHOLE_MIX_INSTRUMENT_TAGGING moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)
# (WHOLE_MIX_INSTRUMENT_TAG_TOP_K moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)
# (WHOLE_MIX_INSTRUMENT_TAG_MIN_PROB moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)
# When a stem claims guitar (etc.) but the whole-mix tagger does not agree
# above this bar, annotate the stem tag as "weak / mix-disagrees" so the
# writer prefers omission over a confident instrument claim.
# (INSTRUMENT_TAG_MIX_AGREE_MIN_PROB moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)

# --- Guitar-family presence probe (absence must be EARNED, not assumed) -----
# The thresholds above are deliberately strict because a false guitar claim is
# expensive. But "did not clear a strict reporting bar" is NOT the same fact as
# "there is no guitar", and the pipeline used to conflate the two: any track
# whose guitar tag failed the 0.35 family bar -- or that simply got squeezed
# out of the whole-mix top-k by six drum/bass/synth labels -- produced a hard
# GUITAR ABSENCE NOTE, which the writer then treated as authoritative. On
# dense, brick-walled modern pop-rock masters (where PANNs routinely scores a
# loud overdriven rhythm guitar in the 0.15-0.30 band, and often reads it as
# synth) that turns a detector limitation into a confident musicological
# error, and then into a wrong genre call, since "no guitar" gets used as an
# argument against a rock/pop-rock reading.
#
# These constants define a SEPARATE, lower-stakes question answered from the
# raw per-window probabilities directly (bypassing top-k and the reporting
# thresholds entirely): is there measurable guitar-family energy anywhere in
# this mix? A confident absence claim now requires positive evidence of
# absence -- low peak AND low persistence AND an inactive Demucs guitar stem.
# Anything in between produces an explicit "inconclusive" note instead.
# (GUITAR_PROBE_ENABLED moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)
# A window counts as "fired" for persistence purposes at/above this.
# (GUITAR_PROBE_FIRE_PROB moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)
# Peak at/above this = treat guitar-family as present (enough to reinstate a
# whole-mix tag that top-k truncation dropped, and to block an absence claim).
# (GUITAR_PROBE_PRESENT_PEAK moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)
# ...or a lower peak that persists across at least this share of windows.
# (GUITAR_PROBE_PRESENT_COVERAGE moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)
# (GUITAR_PROBE_PRESENT_COVERAGE_PEAK moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)
# Confident absence requires the peak to sit below this across the whole track.
# (GUITAR_PROBE_ABSENT_PEAK moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)
# (GUITAR_PROBE_ABSENT_COVERAGE moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)
# Demucs guitar-stem cross-check: htdemucs_6s has a dedicated guitar stem, so
# a genuinely guitar-driven track should leave real energy and real transcribed
# notes there even when the tagger misses it. These are the bars above which
# the stem counts as corroborating evidence against an absence claim.
# (GUITAR_STEM_ACTIVE_MIN_RMS moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)
# (GUITAR_STEM_ACTIVE_MIN_NOTES moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)

# --- Percussion beyond the three transcribed classes ------------------------
# Omnizart's drum model resolves kick, snare and hi-hat and nothing else, so a
# crash, a ride, a tom fill, a tambourine or a shaker is either folded into
# those three or missed outright. That is a hard ceiling on the transcription
# and it cannot be raised without a different model -- but it is NOT a ceiling
# on what the pipeline can know, because the AudioSet tagger has classes for
# exactly the pieces the transcription is blind to. Running it over the
# isolated, peak-normalised drums stem and reading the raw per-window
# probabilities (no top-k, same approach as the guitar probe) recovers the
# missing kit vocabulary, with real coverage figures so extent can be stated
# honestly rather than guessed.
# (DRUM_EXTRAS_PROBE_ENABLED moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)
_DRUM_EXTRA_PROBE_LABELS = (
    "cymbal", "tambourine", "timpani", "drum roll", "percussion (unspecified)",
    "rimshot", "cowbell", "wood block", "maraca/shaker", "conga", "bongo",
    "tabla", "djembe", "castanets", "triangle", "steelpan/steel drum",
    "vibraphone", "marimba/xylophone", "glockenspiel", "chime/wind chime",
    "gong", "drum machine",
)
# A window counts as containing the instrument at/above this.
# (DRUM_EXTRAS_FIRE_PROB moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)
# Report it when it either peaks this high...
# (DRUM_EXTRAS_REPORT_PEAK moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)
# ...or persists across at least this share of windows at the fire threshold.
# (DRUM_EXTRAS_REPORT_COVERAGE moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)

# --- Per-section instrument identity ----------------------------------------
# Instrument TAGS were whole-track only: a label either made the list for the
# whole song or it did not. Energy per section (INTENSITY BY SECTION) says how
# LOUD a stem is in each part, and melody per section says what it plays, but
# nothing said WHICH INSTRUMENT is audible WHERE -- so "when does the organ
# come in", "is there guitar in the bridge", "what's playing in the intro"
# had no evidence behind them and got answered from arrangement habit. The
# windowed probabilities already carry timestamps, so this is a regrouping of
# data the tagger has always produced rather than new inference.
# (INSTRUMENT_SECTION_PRESENCE_ENABLED moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)
# (INSTRUMENT_SECTION_MIN_PROB moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)
# (INSTRUMENT_SECTION_MAX_LABELS moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)

# --- Beat grid diagrams -----------------------------------------------------
# A drum pattern is far clearer as a grid than as prose, but an LLM asked to
# draw one from a list of onset timestamps will mangle both the rhythm and the
# column alignment. So the grid is rendered here, from the measured hits, and
# the writer is told to reproduce it verbatim rather than redraw it.
# (BEAT_GRID_ENABLED moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)
# (BEAT_GRID_BARS moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)
# (BEAT_GRID_SUBDIV moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)
# (BEAT_GRID_MIN_HITS moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)

# --- Cymbal detection from audio (the transcription cannot do it) -----------
# Omnizart resolves kick/snare/hi-hat and nothing else, so a crash or ride is
# either missed or folded into the hi-hat row. The percussion PROBE can say a
# cymbal exists somewhere in a window, but not WHEN, so it cannot fill a grid.
# Cymbals are, however, easy to separate from hats acoustically: both are
# bright, but a closed hat dies in tens of milliseconds while a crash or ride
# rings for hundreds. Measuring how long the high band sustains after each hit
# recovers cymbal ONSETS, which is what a grid row needs.
# Deliberately conservative -- a false cymbal row is worse than a missing one,
# since the grid is presented as measured fact.
# (CYMBAL_ONSET_DETECT_ENABLED moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)
# (CYMBAL_BAND_HZ moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)
# The high band must stay this many times above the track's own median
# between-hits floor to count as still ringing.
# (CYMBAL_FLOOR_RATIO moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)
# ...and that sustained level must be a real fraction of the hit's own peak,
# so a quiet hit sitting inside an earlier cymbal's wash is not counted again.
# (CYMBAL_MIN_SUSTAIN_RATIO moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)
# A struck cymbal must spike this many times above the level just before it.
# Separates a genuine hit from the tail of a previous crash.
# (CYMBAL_MIN_ATTACK_RATIO moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)
# Consecutive detections closer than this are one cymbal ringing, not several.
# (CYMBAL_MERGE_GAP_S moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)
# Give up unless the drums stem yields at least this many, to avoid drawing a
# row from two ambiguous detections.
# (CYMBAL_MIN_DETECTIONS moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)

# --- Arrangement change points for structure boundaries ---------------------
# Acoustic novelty is weakest exactly where pop songs change section: a chorus
# entering on the same pad, key and tempo as the verse produces almost no
# timbral novelty, so the boundary gets placed by whichever nearby spectral
# wobble happened to score highest. But the ARRANGEMENT changes audibly at
# that moment -- the snare arrives, the bass drops out, a pad enters -- and
# that is now measurable, because every stem is transcribed with timestamps.
#
# Measured against a real track, every one of its nine section boundaries had
# an instrument entering or leaving within four seconds. The catch is
# selectivity: isolated single-stem events (a pad swell, a transcription gap)
# outnumber real section changes, while genuine boundaries tend to move
# SEVERAL stems at once. So corroboration count is what separates signal from
# noise here, and single-stem events are deliberately ranked below bar lines.
# (ARRANGEMENT_CHANGE_ENABLED moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)
# Silence longer than this ends an instrument's playing span (also scaled by
# tempo at the call site, as in the drum timeline).
# (ARRANGEMENT_SPAN_GAP_S moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)
# A span shorter than this many bars is a burst or a transcription artifact,
# not an instrument entering the arrangement.
# (ARRANGEMENT_MIN_SPAN_BARS moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)
# Entries/exits within this many bars of each other are one arrangement change.
# (ARRANGEMENT_CLUSTER_BARS moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)
# At least this many distinct stems must move together for a change point to
# be trusted as strongly as an acoustic novelty peak.
# (ARRANGEMENT_STRONG_MIN_STEMS moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)

# --- Low-power whole-mix pass (disagreement needs power to mean anything) ---
# The mix-agreement gate assumes the whole-mix pass is a competent second
# opinion, so "the mix did not agree" is read as evidence against a stem tag.
# On some masters the pass simply fails: it returns one or two labels barely
# over threshold and identifies nothing else in the track. A pass like that has
# no discriminating power, so its disagreement carries no information -- yet it
# was still vetoing every stem tag, which is the same absence-of-evidence
# error the guitar note used to make, one level up. When the mix pass looks
# this weak the gate is suspended: stem tags are annotated as un-corroborated
# rather than downgraded, and the drop rules do not fire.
# (MIX_PASS_LOW_POWER_MAX_TAGS moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)
# (MIX_PASS_LOW_POWER_MAX_TOP_PROB moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)

# --- Cross-stem convergence -------------------------------------------------
# Demucs splits one instrument across several stems (a loud rhythm guitar
# leaves energy in "guitar", "bass" and "other"), so the same instrument being
# tagged independently on multiple stems, each with sustained coverage, is real
# corroboration -- arguably better than the whole-mix pass, since each stem is
# isolated and peak-normalised rather than buried in a limited master. This
# counted for nothing before: three stems could agree on guitar at 64-82% and
# all three would still be marked "stem-only, whole-mix did not agree".
# (CROSS_STEM_CONVERGENCE_MIN_STEMS moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)
# (CROSS_STEM_CONVERGENCE_MIN_PROB moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)
# (CROSS_STEM_CONVERGENCE_MIN_SUSTAINED_STEMS moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)

# Cross-stem agreement is NOT fully independent evidence. All six stems come
# from one separation of one file, so a single timbral confusion propagates
# into every stem it bleeds into, and two stems "agreeing" can be one source
# counted twice. Two guards below.
#
# 1) ANCHORING. Demucs has a dedicated stem for some families, so a guitar tag
#    on the guitar stem, or a bass tag on the bass stem, is that stem's own
#    source rather than bleed. An agreement containing such a native hit is
#    anchored and trustworthy; an agreement made only of non-native stems
#    (e.g. organ found on "other" and "guitar") has no anchor and may be a
#    single mis-identified source appearing twice.
_STEM_NATIVE_FAMILIES = {
    "guitar": ("guitar (type uncertain)", "electric guitar", "acoustic guitar",
               "slide/steel guitar", "strummed chords (guitar family)",
               "guitar technique (tapping)"),
    "bass": ("bass guitar", "upright bass"),
    "piano": ("piano", "electric piano"),
    "vocals": ("choir/vocal ensemble", "chant", "humming", "whistling"),
    "drums": ("drum kit", "drums", "drum machine", "snare", "kick", "hi-hat",
              "cymbal", "percussion (unspecified)", "tambourine", "rimshot",
              "drum roll", "cowbell"),
}
# Stem pairs Demucs routinely bleeds between; agreement across only these,
# with no native anchor, is reported as possible double-counting.
BLEED_PRONE_STEM_PAIRS = frozenset({
    frozenset(("bass", "guitar")),
    frozenset(("guitar", "other")),
    frozenset(("bass", "other")),
    frozenset(("piano", "other")),
    frozenset(("guitar", "piano")),
})

# 2) EXTENT CONFLICT. Presence and extent are different claims. When the
#    whole-mix pass DID detect a label but placed it in only a couple of
#    windows, while the stems claim it runs through the track, the whole-mix
#    read is the one measured without separation artifacts -- so the stems are
#    probably catching a similar timbre (a pad read as organ) rather than the
#    instrument playing throughout. Report the conflict instead of asserting
#    an ongoing part. Requires whole-mix persistence stats, which is why these
#    must survive the probability-matrix eviction (see PANNS_TAG_STATS_CACHE_SIZE).
# (CONVERGENCE_EXTENT_CONFLICT_ENABLED moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)

# --- Objective genre/mood signal (optional) ---------------------------------
# GENRE_RANKED and MOOD_VIBE previously had no independent cross-check at
# all (unlike tempo/key, which are reconciled against Essentia). AudioSet —
# the same label set the instrument tagger already draws from — includes a
# few hundred genre/mood classes (Pop music, Reggae, Bluegrass, Sad music,
# etc.), so the already-loaded PANNs tagger can double as a lightweight,
# broad-category genre/mood classifier with zero extra dependencies. This is
# still just supporting evidence -- AudioSet genre labels are broad,
# overlapping, and derived from noisy YouTube metadata -- not a replacement
# for the writer's own multi-cue GENRE_RANKED judgment.
# (ENABLE_GENRE_MOOD_TAGGING moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)
# (GENRE_TAG_TOP_K moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)
# (GENRE_TAG_MIN_PROB moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)
# (MOOD_TAG_TOP_K moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)
# (MOOD_TAG_MIN_PROB moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)

# --- Demucs separation quality (test-time shift ensembling) -----------------
# `--shifts N` runs Demucs N times on randomly time-shifted copies of the
# input and averages the results, which measurably improves separation
# quality (particularly for the weaker htdemucs_6s guitar/piano stems) at
# a roughly (N+1)x runtime cost. Off by default in fast mode; enabled in
# deep mode, where the user has already signalled they want more accuracy
# over speed (see MOSS_DEEP_MODE_ADDENDUM).
# (DEMUCS_SHIFTS_FAST moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)
# (DEMUCS_SHIFTS_DEEP moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)

# (MAX_IMAGES_PER_REQUEST moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)
# (ENABLE_COVER_ART_DESCRIPTION moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)
# (ENABLE_SINGER_IDENTITY_RESOLUTION moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)
# (ENABLE_IMAGE_OBSERVATIONS_FOR_GENERAL moved to the USER CONFIGURATION section near the top of this file.)

# Rewrite internal vocal-category vocabulary ("post-puberty male", raw
